{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md | sed -n '180,450p'; cat collab/1792506992000-architect-host-boundary-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md; cat collab/1792503557000-architect-host-boundary-review-r2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"   180\treference after a collection, an atom another owned thread advanced) fails\n   181\tnone of the six properties: it is a fact about the machine, not the world.\n   182\tIt is a **runtime prerequisite** (table C), neither IO nor a profile entry.\n   183\tr2 classified it as both; that contradiction is withdrawn.\n   184\t\n   185\t### 2.4 Ownership and mutation laws for the profile\n   186\t\n   187\tThe profile's purity claim is exact, not blanket:\n   188\t\n   189\t- **Pure entries** (marked P in table A) are deterministic, total over their\n   190\t  declared domain, synchronous, and allocate only fresh values; equal\n   191\t  arguments give equal results on every host.\n   192\t- **Mutating entries** (marked M: `bytes/set!`, `bytes/copy!`, `arrays/aset`,\n   193\t  `arrays/acopy`, `arrays/asort`) mutate **exactly the owned argument\n   194\t  named** and nothing else; they return that argument or nil; they never\n   195\t  retain it. The caller must own the argument (it allocated it through the\n   196\t  profile or received it from a profile entry marked \"fresh\"). Overlapping\n   197\t  `copy!` behaves as memmove. Out-of-range indices refuse before any write.\n   198\t- **Aliasing**: `bytes/slice`, `arrays/asub`, `arrays/aclone`, `from-vector`\n   199\t  always return a fresh value; no entry returns a view onto an argument. A\n   200\t  value that crosses a stream is copied by the transport's codec, so no\n   201\t  owned mutable value ever aliases a stream value.\n   202\t- **Ownership transfer**: a profile value handed to a seam (a byte array\n   203\t  appended to a socket) is owned by the seam from that call; the caller may\n   204\t  not mutate it afterwards. This is the one rule connecting the profile to\n   205\t  the boundary.\n   206\t\n   207\t### 2.5 Borderline cases, decided\n   208\t\n   209\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   210\t| Case                              | Reasoning under §2.3                                                                                    | Verdict    |\n   211\t+===================================+=========================================================================================================+============+\n   212\t| UTF-8 decode, CBOR decode, base64 | Fallible, but the failure is a function of the input (property 5 does not hold); deterministic,          | Direct     |\n   213\t| decode, NFC normalization         | synchronous. Malformed input is a refusal value. NFC depends on a Unicode version, which the profile      | call       |\n   214\t|                                   | pins (16.0, V `debruijn.cljc:314`).                                                                     |            |\n   215\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   216\t| Hashing, signing, seed replay in  | Deterministic functions of bytes (and of the seed). The library is linkage. They execute in the same     | Direct     |\n   217\t| a library (BLAKE3, SHA-256,       | instance; for wasm, compiled in, never imported (§7). `sign.cljc:75-87`'s `SecureRandom` proxy replays  | call, in-  |\n   218\t| Ed25519; `sign.cljc:75-87`)       | the given seed bytes and is deterministic (V, R3 correction).                                            | instance   |\n   219\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   220\t| Fresh entropy, identities, key    | Property 3. Deferred design (§9); the profile contains no generator.                                     | Stream;    |\n   221\t| generation (`sign.cljc:114-124`)  |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n   222\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   223\t| Time; scheduling; the JVM blocking| Properties 1, 3, 4. `coordinate.cljc:80` selects `driver.clj:169-206`, which reads the clock and sleeps  | Stream /   |\n   224\t| content driver                    | (V): JVM-lane IO inside a portable composition. Deferred (§9).                                          | driver;    |\n   225\t|                                   |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n   226\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   227\t| The Python parser                 | In-process, synchronous, deterministic over source text and the pinned grammar: internal, direct call.   | Either;    |\n   228\t|                                   | As another process: property 6, a stream. The stage shape (`parser.cljc:65-75`) serves both. Deferred.   | deferred   |\n   229\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   230\t| Same-host file read, lock, fsync, | Properties 1, 2, 5. External though same-host. Deferred (§9).                                            | Stream;    |\n   231\t| rename, directory listing         |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n   232\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   233\t| Weak and soft references, atomics,| Owned runtime state (§2.1). Not IO, not a profile entry: table C. The B-tree's default reference mode is  | Runtime    |\n   234\t| `locking`, atoms                  | `:soft` on the JVM and `:strong` on JS and Dart (V `btree.cljc:39-44`); correctness is independent of    | prereq.    |\n   235\t|                                   | the mode, only retention differs.                                                                       |            |\n   236\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   237\t| Host `=`, `hash`, `compare`,      | Internal and pure per host, not identical across hosts for mixed types and floats. A portability hazard  | Dialect    |\n   238\t| `sort`, `str` of a double, regex  | only where persisted bytes depend on it; those sites sort by canonical bytes (`cbor.cljc:1070,1177`,     | semantics  |\n   239\t|                                   | `ucf/rows.cljc:139`, `debruijn.cljc:921,929`). Regex sites (`custody.cljc:53`, `datagram.cljc:86-100`,   |            |\n   240\t|                                   | `jing.cljc:107-111,216-221`, `fs.cljc:105`) match ASCII grammars identical on every host.                 |            |\n   241\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   242\t| `gensym` in a compiler            | A process-wide counter shared with code the machine does not own: property 3 in a weak form. Deferred    | Deferred   |\n   243\t|                                   | (§9), with the hygiene requirement the r2 review added.                                                 |            |\n   244\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   245\t\n   246\t### 2.6 Where the owner's original sentence is and is not met\n   247\t\n   248\tMet for every external contact: after this design and the deferred one, a\n   249\thost's world contact is the stream seams and its `main`. Not met literally:\n   250\ta port also implements the primitive profile and meets table C, because the\n   251\tdialect's own carriers cannot be supplied by a stream and must not be. The\n   252\tprofile is exact, enumerated and oracle-checked, so that part is a list, not\n   253\ta design.\n   254\t\n   255\t## 3. Classification over the adopted closure\n   256\t\n   257\t### 3.1 The transitive closure, per lane (R, computed)\n   258\t\n   259\tMethod: a script walked `ns` require and import forms from `yin.repl.main`,\n   260\tchoosing each lane's reader arms (`:cljd` also reads `:clj`), resolving\n   261\tnamespaces to files under `src/<lane>` then `src/cljc`, on 2026-10-20. It is\n   262\ta require-form closure, not a compiler-resolved call graph, so it can\n   263\tover-include a namespace required but unused on a path and cannot see\n   264\tdynamic loading; it cannot under-include a static require. Result: 141\n   265\tnamespaces on the JVM lane, 140 on Node, 139 on Dart; 133 are shared `.cljc`.\n   266\t\n   267\tLane-specific members (V, the files exist):\n   268\t\n   269\t+-------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   270\t| Lane  | Members only on this lane                                                                                                               |\n   271\t+=======+=========================================================================================================================================+\n 