`remote.cljc:1249`; `remote_pair.cljc:180`; `remote_route.cljc:173`;\n   341\t`journal.cljc:296`; `dao/jing/content/step.cljc:132`; `dao/lease.cljc:594`;\n   342\t`dao/await.cljc:235,240`; `dao/space/store/fs.cljc:137,351`;\n   343\t`yin/vm/linker.cljc:1685`; `yin/vm/ucf/custody.cljc:46`;\n   344\t`yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc:1507`; `yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve.cljc:417,604,606`;\n   345\t`yin/vm/telemetry.cljc:112`; `yin/repl.cljc:667,668,733,918`;\n   346\t`yin/repl/dht.cljc:117`. Other sources: `rand` at `rpc.cljc:114-116`,\n   347\t`rand-int` at `jing/dht.cljc:373`; secure generators at\n   348\t`space/dht.cljc:142-151` and `sign.cljc:119-123`; `gensym` at three sites;\n   349\tclocks at `main.cljc:1242-1542` (driver) and `driver.clj:169,206` (JVM lane);\n   350\tambient at `fs.cljc:92,136,271-273`, `repl.cljc:275-277`, `main.cljc`;\n   351\tprinting at `main.cljc` (19) and `antlr/repl.cljc:71`; timers at\n   352\t`async.cljc:52-57`, `storage.cljc:224-227`, `main.cljc:1321,1343`. All are\n   353\tdeferred-scope entries (§9) except the clocks and printing in `main.cljc`\n   354\t(driver). None is in an H0-H4 file.\n   355\t\n   356\t### 3.3 Exempt dialect syntax\n   357\t\n   358\tNot host contact; the H0 gate exempts them by pattern: `catch` type arms;\n   359\t`^long`/`^bytes`/`^String` hints; protocol and interface names in `deftype`\n   360\tbodies and `print-method`; `exists?`; `js-obj`/`(Object.)` opaque sentinels;\n   361\trequire arms whose only purpose is an alias.\n   362\t\n   363\t## 4. The host-implementation contract\n   364\t\n   365\t### 4.1 Stream implementations and their seams (external contact)\n   366\t\n   367\tUnchanged from the tree for sockets (V) and listed for completeness; the\n   368\tstorage, entropy and parser rows are deferred (§9).\n   369\t\n   370\t+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   371\t| Implementation             | Portable core (V)                          | Seam value a host supplies                            | Status                      |\n   372\t+============================+============================================+=======================================================+=============================+\n   373\t| Ring buffer, memory log,   | `dao.stream.ringbuffer`, `.memory_log`,    | None. (They mint identities today, §3.2: deferred.)   | Existing                    |\n   374\t| remote channel, waitset,   | `.remote_channel`, `.waitset`, `.observe`, |                                                       |                             |\n   375\t| middleware, apply          | `.middleware`, `.apply`                    |                                                       |                             |\n   376\t+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   377\t| WebSocket                  | `dao.stream.ws`                            | `{:connect! :bind! :unbind!}` (`host.cljc:20-33`)     | Existing                    |\n   378\t+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   379\t| Datagram and UDP           | `dao.stream.datagram`, `.udp`              | `bind!` (`datagram.cljc:15-23`)                       | Existing                    |\n   380\t+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   381\t| Durable journal and the    | `dao.stream.journal` over `dao.jing.file`  | Deferred (§9.2)                                       | Deferred                    |\n   382\t| storage beneath it         | and `store.fs`                             |                                                       |                             |\n   383\t+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   384\t| Entropy                    | Deferred                                   | Deferred (§9.1)                                       | Deferred                    |\n   385\t+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   386\t| Parser stage               | `yang.python.antlr.*`                      | Deferred (§9.3)                                       | Deferred                    |\n   387\t+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   388\t| wasm import surface        | `dao.stream.wasm.md` §1                    | The seven imports                                     | Existing spec               |\n   389\t+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   390\t\n   391\tSeam rule (`abi.md` §6-§7 as acceptance): a seam entry is implementation-\n   392\tprivate, called only by the stream implementation that owns it; it takes\n   393\thost arguments and returns or deposits plain data; it never invokes\n   394\tapplication code; it classifies host errors into data; what it cannot do it\n   395\tanswers as a qualified unsupported outcome. A map of functions that portable\n   396\tapplication code calls to reach the world is a host service and is rejected\n   397\t(`abi.md:143-148`); none exists in H0-H4.\n   398\t\n   399\t### 4.2 The primitive profile: exact manifest (P)\n   400\t\n   401\t`dao.host.*` (OQ2 adopted) is a namespace family whose files contain only\n   402\treader-conditional entries and docstrings stating each entry's law, kind (P\n   403\tpure, M mutating per §2.4) and oracle. Table A is the profile (76 entries);\n   404\ttable B the portable code replacing host libraries; table C the runtime\n   405\tprerequisites.\n   406\t\n   407\t**Table A: profile entries.**\n   408\t\n   409\t+--------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-------+\n   410\t| Namespace          | Entries (consolidated from, V)                                                                       | N   | Slice |\n   411\t+====================+======================================================================================================+=====+=======+\n   412\t| `dao.host.integer` | From `yin.vm.integer.host` (all 25, P): `big-carrier?`, `exact-integer?`, `safe-native?`,            | 28  | H1    |\n   413\t|                    | `host-float?`, `nan-float?`, `infinite-float?`, `promote`, `from-native`, `demote`, `to-native`,     |     |       |\n   414\t|                    | `native->double`, `trunc-native`, `add`, `sub`, `mul`, `quot-trunc`, `rem-trunc`, `compare-big`,      |     |       |\n   415\t|                    | `bit-and-big`, `bit-or-big`, `bit-xor-big`, `shift-left-big`, `shift-right-big`,                     |     |       |\n   416\t|                    | `magnitude-bit-length`, `to-radix-string`; from `cbor.cljc:112-254` (P): `parse-decimal` (digit      |     |       |\n   417\t|                    | string to big, refuses otherwise), `gcd`, `magnitude-bytes` (fresh bytes). `debruijn_code.cljc:315`  |     |       |\n   418\t|                    | reads `big-carrier?` (H2).                                                                           |     |       |\n   419\t+--------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-------+\n   420\t| `dao.host.float`   | P: `float64->bits` (16 hex, big-endian), `bits->float64`, `finite?`, `integral?`, `fields` (sign,    | 5   | H1    |\n   421\t|                    | biased exponent, fraction big) from `cbor.cljc:525-560`, `debruijn.cljc:855-885`,                    |     |       |\n   422\t|                    | `debruijn_code.cljc:232-242` (little-endian hex becomes a portable byte reversal over                |     |       |\n   423\t|                    | `float64->bits`, H2), `data.cljc:77-92`.                                                             |     |       |\n   424\t+--------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-------+\n   425\t| `dao.host.number`  | The Jing numeric carriers and their host construction and access, from `cbor.cljc:342-460,483-632`   | 10  | H1    |\n   426\t|                    | (P): `float64-of`, `float-value`, `decimal?`, `decimal-of` (exponent, mantissa; refuses outside the  |     |       |\n   427\t|                    | JVM scale window, `:552-574`), `decimal-exponent`, `decimal-mantissa`, `ratio?`, `ratio-of`          |     |       |\n   428\t|                    | (reduced, sign on numerator, zero denominator refused), `ratio-numerator`, `ratio-denominator`; the  |     |       |\n   429\t|                    | `Float64`, `Rational`, `Decimal` deftypes move with them (table C laws).                              |     |       |\n   430\t+--------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-------+\n   431\t| `dao.host.bytes`   | P: `bytes?`, `alloc` (fresh, zeroed), `length`, `get`, `slice` (fresh), `equal?`, `from-vector`      | 10  | H1    |\n   432\t|                    | (fresh), `to-vector`; M: `set!`, `copy!` (memmove). From `chunks.cljc:6-32`, `jing/dht.cljc:100-121`,|     |       |\n   433\t|                    | `linker.cljc:1232`, `sign.cljc:55-59`, `boring.cljc:46-51`, `datagram.cljc:63-66`,                   |     |       |\n   434\t|                    | `debruijn.cljc:167`. (`codec.cljc:45` reads it after the codec track's C4, §8.1.)                    |     |       |\n   435\t+--------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-------+\n   436\t| `dao.host.text`    | P: `unit-count`, `code-unit`, `units->string`, `utf8-encode` (fresh bytes; refuses an unpaired       | 6   | H1    |\n   437\t|                    | surrogate), `utf8-decode` (refuses invalid sequences; never substitutes U+FFFD), `nfc` (Unicode 16.0) |     |       |\n   438\t|                    | from `data.cljc:119-173`, `debruijn.cljc:241,310-321`, `jing.cljc:65-71,131-135`, `sign.cljc:62-66`. |     |       |\n   439\t+--------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-------+\n   440\t| `dao.host.hash`    | P: `blake3-bytes`, `sha256-bytes` (`jing.cljc:59-135`; the string forms become portable over `text`).| 2   | H3    |\n   441\t+--------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-------+\n   442\t| `dao.host.sign`    | P: `keypair-from-seed` (`sign.cljc:75-104`, seed replay), `sign`, `verify`. `generate` (fresh         | 3   | H3    |\n   443\t|                    | entropy, `:114-124`) stays in `yin.vm.linker.sign` until the deferred design moves it.               |     |       |\n   444\t+--------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-------+\n   445\t| `dao.data.arrays`  | In place (OQ2). P: `anew` (fresh), `aget`, `alength`, `aclone` (fresh), `asub` (fresh), `aconcat`    | 12  | H2    |\n   446\t| (member by         | (fresh), `ainsert-at` (fresh), `aremove-at` (fresh), `from-coll` (fresh); M: `aset`, `acopy`         |     |       |\n   447\t| reference)         | (memmove), `asort` (stable; `parallelSort` replaced). `arrays.cljc:28-182`.                          |     |       |\n   448\t+--------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-------+\n   449\t\n   450\t**Table B: portable over the profile, no host arm (P).** Base64 (replaces\nCreated-GMT: 2026-10-20 15:10:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 22:10:00 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nModel: claude-fable-5-1\nSession-ID: 36134bec-9cbd-4852-b78c-4901af66fd02\nTask: host-boundary design r3, scope narrowed to the rule plus H0-H4\nRole: Architect\nDeliverable: docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md revised in place (r3); no src edits, nothing staged.\nLabels: V verified by reading, R computed by script or read, P proposed. No lane run. Non-Claude sign-off of the narrowed scope required.\n\n# Rationale\n\n**Scope.** Per \"yes, proceed with the split\", the commissionable scope is §2\nplus H0-H4. H5-H8 form a new §9 \"DEFERRED scope\", not commissionable, with\ntheir r2 intent kept and astra's R2, R5, R6 and A7 recorded as required\ninputs for a separate design, proposed as `dao.stream.host-effects.md`. Slice\nids are reserved so no citation moves.\n\n**R1.** §2.1 defines the machine (the instance and the runtime state it owns)\nagainst the world; §2.3's properties are stated with respect to the world, so\na weak-reference read is owned state, neither IO nor a profile entry, and the\nr2 contradiction is withdrawn. §2.4 gives ownership and mutation laws: entries\nare P or M; M entries mutate exactly the named owned argument; `slice`,\n`asub`, `aclone`, `from-vector` are fresh; `copy!`/`acopy` are memmove;\nownership transfers to a seam on handoff. §12 is relabelled an owner proposal\nwith the owner's words marked and the rest marked proposed.\n\n**R3.** A script walked `ns` forms from `yin.repl.main` per lane: 141 JVM,\n140 Node, 139 Dart namespaces, 133 shared; lane-only members listed; labelled\nR with its limits. Every namespace with host contact is classified with a\nslice or a deferral. Corrections: 27 `random-uuid` lines, 26 in scope;\n`coordinate.cljc:80` selects the JVM blocking driver (clock, sleep), deferred;\n`sign.cljc:75-87` is deterministic seed replay and moves in H3 while\n`generate` stays deferred; regex sites are ASCII grammars.\n\n**R4, A4-A6.** Table A is 76 entries with a new `dao.host.number` (10, from\n`cbor.cljc:483-632`, carriers moving with them); `debruijn_code.cljc:232-242,\n315` assigned to H2; table C corrects the B-tree default to `:soft` on the\nJVM. Table D is the per-entry oracle matrix with file and deftest names;\ncounts corrected (integer 25, sign 4, base64 3 existing). H2's gate: the grep\nlists exactly the shim's own file before deletion, nothing after. H0's final\nlist 1 names the retained arms, so the gate promises \"no arms outside list\n1\", not \"none in src/cljc\".\n\n**Disjointness (V).** H0-H4 source and test files are listed against the\ncodec track's; intersection empty with one deferred one-line edit\n(`codec.cljc:45`). The host-lane probe track's narrow source adaptations\ntouch exactly the H1-H4 files: paused under OQ3, file-level coordination\nneeded. Cost and risk restated for H0-H4; base64 and the writer are\nreplacements, not code motion.\n\n# Disposition\n\n+-----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Id  | Disposition   | Where in r3                                                                                              |\n+=====+===============+==========================================================================================================+\n| R1  | accepted      | §2.1, §2.3, §2.4, §2.5 (weak refs); §12 owner proposal.                                                  |\n+-----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| R2  | deferred      | §9.1 required inputs: allocation, resumable consumption, partial batches, bootstrap identity, UUID       |\n|     |               | spelling, DHT synchronous draws, completions as data.                                                    |\n+-----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| R3  | accepted      | §3.1 per-lane closure and classification; §3.2 counts and sites; §2.5 corrections.                       |\n+-----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| R4  | accepted      | §4.2 table A and C; H2 assignment of `debruijn_code`; H0 list 1 reconciled; H2 greps.                    |\n+-----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| R5  | deferred      | §9.2: pre-rename failure, post-rename visibility, sync uncertainty (`fs.cljc:352-387`).                   |\n+-----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| R6  | modified      | §8.1 source and test allocation and the codec-test dependency; §9.1 H5 re-estimate and UCF migrations;   |\n|     |               | §10 cost and risk for H0-H4.                                                                             |\n+-----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| A4  | accepted      | Table A complete for H0-H4; table C with slices or \"none\".                                               |\n+-----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| A5  | accepted      | Table D matrix; P suites named; corpus errata preserved in H4.                                           |\n+-----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| A6  | accepted      | H2 consumer list; one-file grep before, empty after; H1 direct tests.                                    |\n+-----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| A7  | deferred      | §9.2 lower operations and outcomes as required inputs.                                                   |\n+-----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nNot verified: no lane run; the closure is from require forms, not a compiler;\nH4 performance open until measured.\n**NOT SIGNED OFF.**\n\nRead all 1,021 lines, both reviews, doctrine, and repository evidence, including more than a dozen cited source/oracle files. No edits.\n\n`H` = [dao.stream.host-boundary.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md). Source paths below are relative to `src/`.\n\n- **A1 — Resolved for placement:** §§2.3, 2.4, 7 correctly require in-instance primitives and acknowledge porting beyond streams. ABI §§4–5 and wasm’s seven imports support this; no second ABI is introduced. The operational definition still needs correction below.\n- **A2 — Partly resolved:** §§4.1/4.4 remove callable entropy generators and timer thunks, but explicitly retain consumer callbacks inside `step!`.\n- **A3 — Partly resolved:** §3.2 lists **26** in-scope UUID calls, not 24/25. The 27th is excluded `dao/postgraphics/terminal.cljc:14`. No listed UUID site is missing; counts and broader closure claims are wrong.\n- **A4 — Partly resolved:** §4.3 genuinely enumerates 66 functions, but omits carrier operations and migration assignments; runtime prerequisites do not satisfy the stated final gate.\n- **A5 — Partly resolved:** Aliasing, malformed UTF-8, Unicode version, stability and corpus exceptions are improved. Fixtures exist, but grouped suites are not an entry-to-case coverage matrix. Base64 tests already exist; integer/sign suites contain 25/4 deftests, not 26/5.\n- **A6 — Partly resolved:** H2 names all eight external shim consumers found, including three Python tests. Its pre-deletion empty-grep condition still matches the shim’s own namespace; H1’s direct tests are correctly required.\n- **A7 — Partly resolved:** §4.2 supplies the missing claim-file operations, but its atomic-replacement failure guarantee is unsound.\n- **A8 — Partly resolved:** §§8–11 remove invented performance numbers and record OQ1–OQ6. Cost, risk and file-disjointness remain understated.\n\n| ID | Severity | File:line | Evidence | Correction |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R1 | High | H:169,224,872 | “Equal calls differ” classifies weak-reference reads as IO, then exempts them. Mutable byte/array entries also contradict the profile’s blanket purity claim. | Define the boundary first, distinguish owned runtime state from external facts, and specify mutation/ownership laws. Preserve §12 as an owner proposal; do not present this inconsistent definition as verbatim doctrine. |\n| R2 | Blocking | H:590–610 | Stream cursors are non-destructive (`cljc/dao/stream.cljc:159–173`): independent readers can reuse entropy. No allocation, partial-batch, loss or blocked-resumption protocol is specified. `fresh-id`’s 32 hex characters fail `ucf/custody.cljc:50–56`’s UUID grammar. DHT initialization draws multiple values synchronously (`space/dht.cljc:255–269`). | Specify exclusive allocation and resumable consumption, bootstrap identities, preserved UUID spelling, and DHT failure/continuation behavior. Emit async completions as data; moving callbacks into `step!` does not remove them. |\n| R3 | High | H:318–358 | This is lexical grep, not resolved closure analysis. `jing/coordinate.cljc:80` selects a blocking driver whose `clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj:169–206` reads clocks and sleeps. `sign.cljc:79` is deterministic seed replay, not fresh entropy. Regex also appears in custody/datagram. | Commit a transitive, lane-specific closure with classifications; correct 27 total/26 scoped UUID counts and all residual assignments. |\n| R4 | High | H:507–579 | Missing operations include JVM Decimal/Ratio construction/access (`jing/cbor.cljc:483–632`). `yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:232–245,315` retains float/BigInteger interop without a migration assignment. Btree’s actual default is JVM `:soft` (`btree.cljc:39–44`), not universally strong. | Complete the manifest/runtime mapping and assign migrations. Reconcile retained runtime arms with H0’s final allowlist and impossible H2 greps. |\n| R5 | High | H:476–477 | `store/fs.cljc:367–385` renames before directory sync; sync failure can leave new HEAD visible. | Distinguish pre-rename failure, replacement visibility and durability uncertainty; test post-rename failure explicitly. |\n| R6 | High | H:744–775 | Mandatory identities require edits to codec-owned tests: `ws_test.cljc:11`, `remote_route_test.cljc:24` construct identity-less rings. §9 also says UCF untouched despite H5 custody/holder migrations. | Allocate source **and test** files, provide staged compatibility or dependencies, and re-estimate H5. Base64 replacement and new refusal laws are not verbatim code motion. |\n\nJ1 arm-adding should wait. J0b/J0c probes can proceed; J0a is **not wholly unaffected**—its source adaptations require coordination.\n\nOQ7/OQ8 appropriately seek owner decisions. OQ9 alternatives both can avoid core generators; its recommendation overclaims exclusivity. OQ10 may ask about accepting a residual, but compiler counters need hygienic naming, not merely resetting numbering; transaction `gensym` needs separate treatment.\n\nNot verified: executable lane parity, performance, crash/race behavior, exhaustive resolved closure/oracle coverage, or produced wasm imports. These remain proposed gates, not demonstrated properties.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
