{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'closure|python3|141|read-cond' collab/1792506992000-architect-host-boundary-r3.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log | head -35; sed -n '1,240p' src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc; sed -n '1,190p' src/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc; sed -n '28,125p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc; sed -n '1,135p' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"8:- **R3.** A script computed the per-lane require closure from the REPL main: 141 namespaces on the JVM, 140 on Node, 139 on Dart, labelled as computed and not compiler-resolved. Every namespace with host contact is classified with a slice or a deferral. Counts corrected to 27 `random-uuid` lines with 26 in scope. The JVM blocking content driver and the deterministic seed replay in signing are reclassified as astra found.\n(ns dao.stream.ringbuffer\n  \"Fixed-capacity, evict-oldest DaoStream v2 reference transport.\"\n  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]))\n\n\n(def transport-type :dao.stream/ringbuffer)\n(def capacity-key :dao.stream.ringbuffer/capacity)\n\n\n(defn- result\n  [outcome]\n  {:dao.stream/outcome outcome})\n\n\n(defn- valid-spec?\n  [spec]\n  (and (map? spec)\n       (= transport-type (:dao.stream/type spec))\n       (integer? (get spec capacity-key))\n       (pos? (get spec capacity-key))))\n\n\n(defn- fresh-state\n  [capacity]\n  ;; The logical identity is carried in descriptors and cursors.  It therefore\n  ;; belongs to DaoStream's portable data domain, unlike a host UUID object.\n  (atom {:identity (str (random-uuid))\n         :capacity capacity\n         :first 0\n         :tail 0\n         :values {}\n         :closed? false\n         :attachments {}}))\n\n\n(declare attach!)\n\n\n(deftype RingHandle\n  [state attachment-id owner?]\n\n  stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\n\n  (descriptor\n    [_]\n    (let [s @state]\n      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n       :dao.stream/descriptor {:dao.stream/type transport-type\n                               :dao.stream/identity (:identity s)}\n       :dao.stream/identity (:identity s)}))\n\n\n  stream/IDaoStreamReader\n\n  (cursor\n    [_ anchor]\n    (let [s @state]\n      (if (and (not owner?) (not (get-in s [:attachments attachment-id :open?])))\n        (result :dao.stream/closed)\n        (case anchor\n          :dao.stream/oldest (assoc (result :dao.stream/ok)\n                                    :dao.stream/cursor\n                                    {:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity (:identity s)\n                                     :dao.stream.ringbuffer/position (:first s)})\n          :dao.stream/newest (assoc (result :dao.stream/ok)\n                                    :dao.stream/cursor\n                                    {:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity (:identity s)\n                                     :dao.stream.ringbuffer/position (:tail s)})\n          (result :dao.stream/invalid-anchor)))))\n\n\n  (next\n    [_ cursor-value]\n    (let [s @state]\n      (cond\n        (not (map? cursor-value)) (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\n        (not (contains? cursor-value :dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity))\n        (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\n        (not= (:identity s) (:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity cursor-value))\n        (result :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch)\n        (not (integer? (:dao.stream.ringbuffer/position cursor-value)))\n        (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\n        :else\n        (let [pos (:dao.stream.ringbuffer/position cursor-value)\n              frozen (when-not owner? (get-in s [:attachments attachment-id :tail]))\n              visible-tail (if (some? frozen) frozen (:tail s))]\n          (cond\n            (< pos (:first s))\n            (assoc (result :dao.stream/gap)\n                   :dao.stream/cursor\n                   {:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity (:identity s)\n                    ;; The contract's recovery position is the earliest retained\n                    ;; position, which equals the tail when nothing is retained.\n                    ;; It must never be an evicted position: a frozen attachment\n                    ;; whose visible history is fully evicted would otherwise be\n                    ;; handed its own tail back and re-gap forever.  Recovering\n                    ;; past that tail is correct; the next read answers `end`.\n                    :dao.stream.ringbuffer/position (:first s)})\n            (< pos visible-tail)\n            ;; `:first`, `:tail`, and `:values` are updated as one state value;\n            ;; positions in this interval are always retained by the transport.\n            {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n             :dao.stream/value (get (:values s) pos)\n             :dao.stream/cursor {:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity (:identity s)\n                                 :dao.stream.ringbuffer/position (inc pos)}}\n            (or (:closed? s) (some? frozen)) (result :dao.stream/end)\n            :else (result :dao.stream/blocked))))))\n\n\n  stream/IDaoStreamWriter\n\n  (append!\n    [_ value]\n    ;; Check attachment liveness *inside* the one state transition.  A deref\n    ;; before swap! permits an append that began before an attachment close to\n    ;; land after it; this form gives append!/close! a single linearization point.\n    (let [out (volatile! nil)]\n      (swap! state\n             (fn [s]\n               (if (or (:closed? s)\n                       (and (not owner?)\n                            (not (get-in s [:attachments attachment-id :open?]))))\n                 (do (vreset! out (result :dao.stream/closed)) s)\n                 (let [p (:tail s) n (inc p)\n                       first' (max (:first s) (- n (:capacity s)))]\n                   (vreset! out (result :dao.stream/ok))\n                   (-> s (assoc :tail n :first first')\n                       (assoc :values\n                              (assoc (if (> first' (:first s))\n                                       (dissoc (:values s) (:first s))\n                                       (:values s)) p value)))))))\n      @out))\n\n\n  stream/IDaoStreamClosable\n\n  (close!\n    [_]\n    (swap! state\n           (fn [s]\n             (if owner?\n               (assoc s :closed? true)\n               ;; Preserve the first frozen tail.  Later idempotent closes must\n               ;; not make post-close values visible through this attachment.\n               (if (get-in s [:attachments attachment-id :open?])\n                 (assoc-in s [:attachments attachment-id]\n                           {:open? false :tail (:tail s)})\n                 s))))\n    (result :dao.stream/ok)))\n\n\n(defn create!\n  \"Create a ring buffer from a creation specification.\"\n  [spec]\n  (if-not (valid-spec? spec)\n    (result :dao.stream/invalid-spec)\n    (let [state (fresh-state (get spec capacity-key))]\n      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n       :dao.stream/handle (RingHandle. state nil true)\n       :dao.stream/identity (:identity @state)})))\n\n\n(defn- resolve-state\n  [resolver identity]\n  (let [x (if (fn? resolver) (resolver identity) (get resolver identity))]\n    (cond (instance? RingHandle x) (.-state ^RingHandle x)\n          #?(:cljd false\n             :clj (instance? clojure.lang.IAtom x)\n             :cljs (instance? cljs.core/Atom x)) x\n          :else nil)))\n\n\n(defn attach!\n  \"Attach using a host-owned resolver (identity -> handle/state).\"\n  [resolver descriptor]\n  (if-not (and (map? descriptor)\n               (= transport-type (:dao.stream/type descriptor))\n               (contains? descriptor :dao.stream/identity))\n    (result :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor)\n    (if-let [state (resolve-state resolver (:dao.stream/identity descriptor))]\n      ;; A resolver is host composition, not a trusted decoder.  Confirm that\n      ;; its result actually denotes the descriptor's identity before minting a\n      ;; handle; otherwise a mis-keyed directory silently attaches elsewhere.\n      (if (= (:dao.stream/identity descriptor) (:identity @state))\n        (let [id (str (random-uuid))]\n          (swap! state assoc-in [:attachments id] {:open? true})\n          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n           :dao.stream/handle (RingHandle. state id false)\n           :dao.stream/attachment id})\n        (result :dao.stream/not-found))\n      (result :dao.stream/not-found))))\n\n\n(defn make-attacher\n  \"Return a unary attach! closure over host-owned resolver state.\n   This small directory is composition/test infrastructure, never a registry\n   owned by DaoStream or this namespace.\"\n  [resolver]\n  (fn [descriptor] (attach! resolver descriptor)))\n(ns dao.data.arrays\n  \"Host-array manipulation layer for dao.data.btree\n  (docs/design/dao.data.btree.md §3.2).\n\n  Node fields hold exactly these host arrays — Object[] on the JVM,\n  js/Array on ClojureScript, List on ClojureDart — so the node types and\n  this layer cannot drift apart.\n\n  `acopy` takes (src src-pos dest dest-pos len), the System/arraycopy\n  shape. This is a deliberate departure from psset's end-exclusive\n  (from from-start from-end to to-start) — see §3.3.3 of the design doc.\n\n  Index/length params carry JVM-only `^long` hints (`#?(:cljd x :clj ^long x\n  :cljs x)` — `:cljd` MUST come first: ClojureDart's host-eval pass also\n  matches `:clj` branches, so a `:clj`/`:default` pair leaks the JVM tag\n  into cljd and fails with \\\"Can't resolve type long\\\"; elided to a plain\n  symbol on cljs/cljd, where the tag is inert metadata off the JVM, never\n  a coercion, so this changes nothing there once ordered correctly). JFR\n  profiling of dao.data.btree (docs/design/dao.data.btree.md §6 Phase 3\n  benchmark record) found ~80% of the tree's own `java.lang.Long`\n  allocation traced to `search` boxing its loop-control longs at every\n  call boundary into this namespace; hinting the boundary here is\n  required for the hint at the call site to actually stick — an unhinted\n  `aget`/`aset`/etc. re-boxes on the way in.\"\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [aget aset alength aclone]))\n\n\n(defn anew\n  \"A new host array of n slots, all nil.\"\n  [#?(:cljd n\n      :clj ^long n\n      :cljs n)]\n  #?(:cljd (object-array (int n))\n     :clj (object-array (int n))\n     :cljs (let [a (js/Array. n)]\n             (.fill a nil)\n             a)))\n\n\n(defn aget\n  [arr\n   #?(:cljd i\n      :clj ^long i\n      :cljs i)]\n  #?(:cljd (clojure.core/aget arr (int i))\n     :clj (clojure.core/aget ^objects arr (int i))\n     :cljs (clojure.core/aget arr i)))\n\n\n(defn aset\n  [arr\n   #?(:cljd i\n      :clj ^long i\n      :cljs i) v]\n  #?(:cljd (clojure.core/aset arr (int i) v)\n     :clj (clojure.core/aset ^objects arr (int i) v)\n     :cljs (clojure.core/aset arr i v)))\n\n\n(defn alength\n  #?(:cljd [arr]\n     :clj ^long [arr]\n     :cljs [arr])\n  #?(:cljd (clojure.core/alength arr)\n     :clj (clojure.core/alength ^objects arr)\n     :cljs (clojure.core/alength arr)))\n\n\n(defn aclone\n  [arr]\n  #?(:cljd (clojure.core/aclone arr)\n     :clj (clojure.core/aclone ^objects arr)\n     :cljs (.slice arr)))\n\n\n(defn acopy\n  \"Copy len slots from src[src-pos ...] into dest[dest-pos ...]. Safe for\n   overlapping ranges within the same array. 5 params: over Clojure's\n   4-arg ceiling for primitive-typed args (\\\"fns taking primitives support\n   only 4 or fewer args\\\"), so this stays boxed at its own boundary — its\n   per-call cost is amortized over the copy length, unlike the per-element\n   cost in search's own arithmetic, which is where the hinting actually\n   pays off (see the ns docstring). stitch-all/stitch-one in\n   dao.data.btree carry their running offset on a StitchAt deftype's\n   `^long` field instead — a different mechanism, not this one, since\n   they hit the same 4-arg ceiling `acopy` does and a name-tagged\n   return-only hint (the escape hatch that works for `acopy`-arity\n   functions in general) turned out to produce no primitive interface\n   for a function with zero primitive-tagged args (verified via\n   `(supers (class f))`).\"\n  [src src-pos dest dest-pos len]\n  #?(:clj (System/arraycopy ^objects src\n                            (int src-pos)\n                            ^objects dest\n                            (int dest-pos)\n                            (int len))\n     :default (if (and (identical? src dest) (> dest-pos src-pos))\n                (loop [i (dec len)]\n                  (when (>= i 0)\n                    (aset dest (+ dest-pos i) (aget src (+ src-pos i)))\n                    (recur (dec i))))\n                (loop [i 0]\n                  (when (< i len)\n                    (aset dest (+ dest-pos i) (aget src (+ src-pos i)))\n                    (recur (inc i)))))))\n\n\n(defn asub\n  \"New array holding arr[from ... to).\"\n  [arr\n   #?(:cljd from\n      :clj ^long from\n      :cljs from)\n   #?(:cljd to\n      :clj ^long to\n      :cljs to)]\n  (let [n (- to from)\n        r (anew n)]\n    (acopy arr from r 0 n)\n    r))\n\n\n(defn aconcat\n  \"New array holding a[0 ... a-len) followed by b[0 ... b-len).\"\n  [a\n   #?(:cljd a-len\n      :clj ^long a-len\n      :cljs a-len) b\n   #?(:cljd b-len\n      :clj ^long b-len\n      :cljs b-len)]\n  (let [r (anew (+ a-len b-len))]\n    (acopy a 0 r 0 a-len)\n    (acopy b 0 r a-len b-len)\n    r))\n\n\n(defn asort\n  \"Sort arr in place by cmp; returns arr. JVM uses parallelSort (matching\n   psset's arrays.cljc): the only call site is from-sequential's one-time\n   bulk sort, ForkJoinPool.commonPool()-parallel work with no correctness\n   difference from a serial sort.\"\n  [arr cmp]\n  #?(:cljd (do (.sort ^List arr cmp) arr)\n     :clj (do (java.util.Arrays/parallelSort ^objects arr\n                                             ^java.util.Comparator cmp)\n              arr)\n     :cljs (do (.sort arr cmp) arr)))\n\n\n(defn ainsert-at\n  \"New array of arr[0 ... len) with x inserted at idx (result length len+1).\"\n  [arr\n   #?(:cljd len\n      :clj ^long len\n      :cljs len)\n   #?(:cljd idx\n      :clj ^long idx\n      :cljs idx) x]\n  (let [r (anew (inc len))]\n    (acopy arr 0 r 0 idx)\n    (aset r idx x)\n    (acopy arr idx r (inc idx) (- len idx))\n    r))\n\n\n(defn aremove-at\n  \"New array of arr[0 ... len) with slot idx removed (result length len-1).\"\n  [arr\n   #?(:cljd len\n      :clj ^long len\n      :cljs len)\n   #?(:cljd idx\n      :clj ^long idx\n      :cljs idx)]\n  (let [r (anew (dec len))]\n    (acopy arr 0 r 0 idx)\n    (acopy arr (inc idx) r idx (- len (inc idx)))\n    r))\n\n\n(defn from-coll\n  \"Host array holding the elements of coll.\"\n  [coll]\n  #?(:cljd (object-array coll)\n     :clj (object-array coll)\n     :cljs (into-array coll)))\n\n\n(def EARLY-EXIT\n  \"Returned when a transient edited in place, nothing to propagate upward.\n   Unreachable in Phase 1 (edit lease is always nil).\"\n  (arr/anew 0))\n\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; Settings (§3.1)\n\n(def ^:private default-ref-type\n  ;; §5.3: :soft on the JVM (memory-pressure-aware), :strong on cljs/cljd\n  ;; where :soft would silently degrade to :weak.\n  #?(:cljd :strong\n     :clj :soft\n     :cljs :strong))\n\n\n(deftype Settings\n  [branching-factor ; max keys per node, default 512\n   ref-type         ; :strong | :soft | :weak | :test;\n   ;; default per host (§5.3)\n   edit             ; transient mutation lease (Phase 3);\n   ;; nil when persistent\n   storage-box])    ; nil, or a volatile holding the IStorage faulting reads\n;; go through (Phase 4). A box, not a value: the\n;; storage\n;; and the settings reference each other, so the slot\n;; is\n;; filled after construction. Plumbing, not identity —\n;; the conceptual Settings shape stays §3.1's three.\n\n(defn- make-settings\n  ^Settings [opts]\n  (Settings. (or (:branching-factor opts) 512)\n             (or (:ref-type opts) default-ref-type)\n             nil\n             nil))\n\n\n(defn- settings-storage\n  [^Settings sett]\n  (some-> (.-storage-box sett)\n          deref))\n\n\n(defn- bf*\n  [^Settings s]\n  (.-branching-factor s))\n\n\n(defn- min-bf*\n  [^Settings s]\n  (quot (.-branching-factor s) 2))\n\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; Edit lease (§3.1, Phase 3). The token is host-appropriate: an\n;; AtomicBoolean on the JVM (transients may be handed between threads, so\n;; the staleness read must be atomic), a plain mutable box on cljs/cljd\n;; (single-threaded / isolate-local). A node is editable in place iff its\n;; own `settings` carries a live lease — nodes from before the transient\n;; began hold different Settings and are never mutated (ownership rule).\n\n#?(:cljd (deftype EditBox\n           [^:mutable val])\n   :clj nil\n   :cljs (deftype EditBox\n           [^:mutable val]))\n\n\n(defn- new-lease\n  []\n  #?(:cljd (EditBox. true)\n     :clj (java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean. true)\n     :cljs (EditBox. true)))\n\n\n(defn- editing?\n  [^Settings sett]\n  (when-some [e (.-edit sett)]\n    #?(:cljd (.-val ^EditBox e)\n       :clj (.get ^java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean e)\n       :cljs (.-val ^EditBox e))))\n\n\n(defn- clear-lease!\n  [^Settings sett]\n  (let [e (.-edit sett)]\n    #?(:cljd (set! (.-val ^EditBox e) false)\n       :clj (.set ^java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean e false)\n       :cljs (set! (.-val ^EditBox e) false))))\n\n\n(defn- editable-settings\n  \"A fresh Settings sharing bf/ref-type/storage with sett, carrying a live\n   lease.\"\n(ns yin.vm.linker.sign\n  \"Ed25519 proofs for canonical module envelopes and published head\n   traces, each under its own domain-separation prefix.\"\n  (:require [clojure.edn :as edn]\n            [clojure.string :as str]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            #?@(:cljd [[\"dart:convert\" :as convert]\n                       [\"package:ed25519_edwards/ed25519_edwards.dart\" :as ed]])\n            #?@(:cljs [[goog.crypt :as crypt]]))\n  #?@(:cljd [(:import [\"dart:typed_data\" Uint8List])])\n  #?@(:cljd []\n      :clj [(:import [java.security KeyFactory KeyPairGenerator\n                      SecureRandom Signature]\n                     [java.security.spec NamedParameterSpec\n                      PKCS8EncodedKeySpec X509EncodedKeySpec])]))\n\n\n(def ^:private domain \"yin.module/envelope:v1\\n\")\n\n\n;; A published head trace's prefix (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 5.2): domain\n;; separation, so a name envelope's signature is never a head proof and a\n;; head proof never a name envelope's.\n(def ^:private head-domain \"yin.head/trace:v1\\n\")\n(def ^:private private-prefix \"302e020100300506032b657004220420\")\n(def ^:private public-prefix \"302a300506032b6570032100\")\n(def ^:private digits \"0123456789abcdef\")\n\n\n(defn- hex?\n  [n s]\n  (and (string? s)\n       (= n (count s))\n       (every? #(some? (str/index-of digits (str %))) s)))\n\n\n(defn- from-hex\n  [s]\n  (mapv (fn [[a b]]\n          (+ (* 16 (str/index-of digits (str a)))\n             (str/index-of digits (str b))))\n        (partition 2 s)))\n\n\n(defn- to-hex\n  [xs]\n  (apply str (mapcat (fn [x]\n                       (let [n (bit-and 255 x)]\n                         [(nth digits (quot n 16))\n                          (nth digits (mod n 16))]))\n                     xs)))\n\n\n(defn- host-bytes\n  [xs]\n  #?(:cljd (Uint8List.fromList (vec xs))\n     :clj (byte-array (map unchecked-byte xs))\n     :cljs (js/Uint8Array.from (clj->js xs))))\n\n\n(defn- utf8\n  [s]\n  #?(:cljd (convert/utf8.encode s)\n     :clj (.getBytes ^String s \"UTF-8\")\n     :cljs (crypt/stringToUtf8ByteArray s)))\n\n\n(defn- message\n  ([canonical] (message domain canonical))\n  ([prefix canonical]\n   (host-bytes (concat (utf8 prefix) canonical))))\n\n\n#?(:cljd nil :clj\n   (defn- jvm-pair\n     [seed]\n     (let [bytes (host-bytes (from-hex seed))\n           random (proxy [SecureRandom] []\n                    (nextBytes\n                      [dst]\n                      (System/arraycopy bytes 0 dst 0 (alength dst))))\n           ;; SunEC's generator draws the 32 seed bytes from this random.\n           ;; The RFC vectors guard that provider-specific dependency.\n           generator (KeyPairGenerator/getInstance \"Ed25519\" \"SunEC\")]\n       (.initialize generator (NamedParameterSpec. \"Ed25519\") random)\n       (.generateKeyPair generator))))\n\n\n(defn public-of\n  [seed]\n  (when-not (hex? 64 seed)\n    (throw (ex-info \"Invalid Ed25519 seed\" {:reason :invalid-seed})))\n  #?(:cljd (to-hex (.-bytes (ed/public\n                              (ed/newKeyFromSeed\n                                (host-bytes (from-hex seed))))))\n     :clj (to-hex (take-last 32 (.getEncoded (.getPublic (jvm-pair seed)))))\n     :cljs (let [crypto (js/require \"crypto\")\n                 der (js/Buffer.from (str private-prefix seed) \"hex\")\n                 private (.createPrivateKey crypto #js {:key der :format \"der\"\n                                                        :type \"pkcs8\"})\n                 public (.createPublicKey crypto private)]\n             (to-hex (take-last 32 (.export public\n                                            #js {:format \"der\" :type \"spki\"}))))))\n\n\n(defn principal\n  [public]\n  (when-not (hex? 64 public)\n    (throw (ex-info \"Invalid Ed25519 public key\" {:reason :invalid-public})))\n  (str \"ed25519:\" public))\n\n\n(defn generate\n  []\n  #?(:cljd (let [pair (ed/generateKey)]\n             {:seed (to-hex (take 32 (.-bytes (.-privateKey pair))))\n              :public (to-hex (.-bytes (.-publicKey pair)))})\n     :clj (let [seed (byte-array 32)]\n            (.nextBytes (SecureRandom.) seed)\n            (let [hex (to-hex seed)]\n              {:seed hex :public (public-of hex)}))\n     :cljs (let [seed (to-hex (.randomBytes (js/require \"crypto\") 32))]\n             {:seed seed :public (public-of seed)})))\n\n\n(defn- sign-bytes\n  [seed bytes]\n  #?(:cljd (ed/sign (ed/newKeyFromSeed (host-bytes (from-hex seed)))\n                    (host-bytes bytes))\n     :clj (let [factory (KeyFactory/getInstance \"Ed25519\" \"SunEC\")\n                encoded (host-bytes (from-hex (str private-prefix seed)))\n                private (.generatePrivate factory (PKCS8EncodedKeySpec. encoded))\n                signer (Signature/getInstance \"Ed25519\" \"SunEC\")]\n            (.initSign signer private)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"The deferred requirements are preserved in section 9, and H0–H4 can retain the existing entropy and scheduling paths. Two in-scope claims need closer checking: the final allowlist appears to omit retained B-tree runtime arms, and the ownership rule assumes every stream copies values through a codec.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\nfiles=['test/yin/vm/integer_test.cljc','test/yin/vm/linker/sign_test.cljc','test/dao/stream/base64_test.cljc','test/yin/vm/data_test.cljc','test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc','test/dao/jing/cbor_fixtures_test.cljc','test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc','test/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc','test/dao/jing_test.cljc','test/dao/jing/cbor_test.cljc','test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc','test/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc','test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc']\nfor f in files:\n s=Path(f).read_text(); names=re.findall(r'\"'^'\"\\\\(deftest\\\\s+(\\\\S+)',s,re.M)\n print(f, 'COUNT',len(names),'CASES', ' '.join(names))\n starts=list(re.finditer(r'\"'^'\"\\\\(deftest',s,re.M))\n for m in starts[:1]: print(s[m.start():m.start()+850])\nPY\nrg -n 'random-uuid' src/cljc\nrg -n 'yin\\\\.vm\\\\.integer\\\\.host' src test\nsed -n '650,790p' docs/design/dao.stream.codec.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\nfiles=['test/yin/vm/integer_test.cljc','test/yin/vm/linker/sign_test.cljc','test/dao/stream/base64_test.cljc','test/yin/vm/data_test.cljc','test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc','test/dao/jing/cbor_fixtures_test.cljc','test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc','test/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc','test/dao/jing_test.cljc','test/dao/jing/cbor_test.cljc','test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc','test/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc','test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc']\nfor f in files:\n s=Path(f).read_text(); names=re.findall(r'\"'^'\"\\\\(deftest\\\\s+(\\\\S+)',s,re.M)\n print(f, 'COUNT',len(names),'CASES', ' '.join(names))\n starts=list(re.finditer(r'\"'^'\"\\\\(deftest',s,re.M))\n for m in starts[:1]: print(s[m.start():m.start()+850])\nPY\nrg -n 'random-uuid' src/cljc\nrg -n 'yin\\\\.vm\\\\.integer\\\\.host' src test\nsed -n '650,790p' docs/design/dao.stream.codec.md\"","aggregated_output":"zsh:1: can't create temp file for here document: operation not permitted\nsrc/cljc/dao/postgraphics/terminal.cljc:14:     :cljs (str (random-uuid))\nsrc/cljc/dao/await.cljc:235:                                                (str (random-uuid)))\nsrc/cljc/dao/await.cljc:240:                                              (str (random-uuid))))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/journal.cljc:296:       (let [i (str (random-uuid))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:27:  (atom {:identity (str (random-uuid))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:185:        (let [id (str (random-uuid))]\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote_pair.cljc:180:                        (assoc policy :dao.stream.remote/request-prefix (str (random-uuid))\nsrc/cljc/dao/lease.cljc:594:  (str (random-uuid)))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote_route.cljc:173:  (let [opts (merge {:dao.stream.remote/request-prefix (str (random-uuid))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc:73:  (atom {:identity (str (random-uuid))\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/store/fs.cljc:137:           own (str lock-name \".\" own-pid \".\" (random-uuid))\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/store/fs.cljc:351:         temp (str dir \"/\" name \".tmp-\" (random-uuid))]\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:122:  (str (random-uuid)))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:1249:                :dao.stream/attachment (str (random-uuid))})))))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc:132:    (let [id (str (random-uuid))]\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:667:             :capability-secret (str (random-uuid))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:668:             :secret-source (fn [_origin] (str (random-uuid)))}\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:733:    (macro/make-ctx {:token (str (random-uuid))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:918:         shell-token (str (random-uuid))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:1685:    (let [id (str (random-uuid))]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc:1507:  (str (random-uuid)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc:117:  (let [end (keyword \"yin.repl.dht\" (str \"end-\" (random-uuid)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/telemetry.cljc:112:      (some? config) (assoc :vm-id (or (:vm-id config) (str (random-uuid))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/custody.cljc:46:  (str (random-uuid)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve.cljc:417:                gid (str (random-uuid))]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve.cljc:604:                    rid (str (random-uuid))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve.cljc:606:                       :identity (str (random-uuid))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/integer/host.cljc:1:(ns yin.vm.integer.host\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:38:            [yin.vm.integer.host :as integer-host]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:41:    [yin.vm.integer.host :as integer-host]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:14:   (`yin.vm.integer.host`); every integer result is in its one canonical\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:101:    [yin.vm.integer.host :as host]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/values.cljc:30:            [yin.vm.integer.host :as integer-host]))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:32:            [yin.vm.integer.host :as integer-host]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_heap_test.cljc:20:            [yin.vm.integer.host :as integer-host]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_ops_test.cljc:18:            [yin.vm.integer.host :as integer-host]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_literal_test.cljc:33:            [yin.vm.integer.host :as host]\npreserves metadata and list-hood where Transit drops them (§4.4); there are\nno deployed peers or stores to stay compatible with. Against: Transit-JSON\nframes are readable in a browser devtools pane; Transit admits identifiers\nand metadata CBOR refuses, so a composition that sends those today would\nstart seeing `invalid-value`; wire size and speed are unmeasured either way.\nThe decision changes one composition value and the cross-host peer suites'\nconfiguration; it is recorded in §10 when made.\n\n## 7. Migration\n\n### 7.1 Slices: expand, migrate, contract\n\nReview finding A2 showed the earlier sequence could not land green slice by\nslice: removing defaults and renaming keys in one step while `remote-channel`,\nthe host adapters and `dao.jing.stream` still read the old keys would have\nbroken production compositions and turned Jing's name test into a nil-to-nil\ncomparison that selects the CBOR branch for Transit. The sequence is\nrestructured as expand (add, nothing removed), migrate (callers switch),\ncontract (remove). Each slice lands on master alone, under the three-lane\nquick tier, with recorded test counts before and after and with the frozen\nwire fixtures of §7.3 passing. \"Counts unchanged\" means the same number of\ntests and assertions per lane, with any added test listed by name.\n\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Slice | Phase    | Change                                                                      | Falsifiable acceptance                                              |\n+=======+==========+=============================================================================+=====================================================================+\n| F0    | freeze   | Record the frozen wire fixtures (§7.3) from the tree **before** any other   | Fixture files committed; the fixture test passes on all three       |\n|       |          | slice, at a tagged master commit. No `src/` change.                         | lanes against unchanged code; the tagged commit is named in the     |\n|       |          |                                                                             | slice report as the \"old peer\" for §7.3's bidirectional check.      |\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| C0    | expand   | Create `dao.stream.codec`: accessors with dual lookup, `profile?` accepting | Three lanes green; counts unchanged plus the named new tests in     |\n|       |          | either complete key set, `safe-number?` and bounds moved from Transit, the  | `codec_test.cljc`: the §4.4 law corpus over both profiles **minus   |\n|       |          | D predicate with `validate-portable`, `host-bytes?` (moved from             | the baseline exception paths of §7.3** (today: Transit lists, which |\n|       |          | `cbor/byte-payload?`, which stays as a forwarding def). Both profiles gain  | round-trip on CBOR and are asserted to keep their frozen per-host   |\n|       |          | the six `:dao.stream.codec/*` keys **beside** the five `:ws/*` keys.        | Transit outcome; §4.4 ruling R2), the adversarial identifier and    |\n|       |          | `dao.stream.cbor`, `dao.stream.transit` and `dao.data` require the          | metadata cases (collection and symbol metadata, direct and nested), |\n|       |          | contract; `cbor.cljc` drops its Transit require. No consumer changes.       | the dual-key gate. `transit-domain-stays-a-subset-of-the-cbor-      |\n|       |          | Transit's decoder is **not** touched, even where it breaks the law (R2).    | domain` is replaced by the law test (its claim was false in         |\n|       |          |                                                                             | general, §4.4). `grep dao.stream.transit src/` no longer hits       |\n|       |          |                                                                             | `cbor.cljc` or `data.cljc`. Fixtures pass. No behaviour change      |\n|       |          |                                                                             | anywhere.                                                           |\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| C1    | expand   | Consumers read profiles only through the accessors, keeping every default:  | Three lanes green; counts unchanged plus: `jing/stream_test`        |\n|       |          | `dao.stream.ws` (gate, `codec-index`, `distinct?` check, frame kind,        | cases for nil, `{}` and a **malformed** profile (a key missing,     |\n|       |          | encode/decode calls), the four host adapters (negotiation and the           | `bytes?` absent or non-boolean, a non-token name), all rejected     |\n|       |          | subprotocol they put on the socket), `dao.jing.stream` (`profile?` then     | before any lane branch; and, positively, a **complete profile       |\n|       |          | `bytes?`; drops both codec requires). `remote-channel` **accepts**          | under an unfamiliar name** (a stub codec the test defines, never    |\n|       |          | optional `:codecs`/`:codec` and threads them to endpoint, bind and          | Transit's or CBOR's name) run twice, with `bytes?` `true` and with  |\n|       |          | attacher; absent, the `ws` default still applies.                           | `bytes?` `false`, asserting the bytes branch and the octets branch  |\n|       |          |                                                                             | respectively, so dispatch is proved to be by capability and not by  |\n|       |          |                                                                             | name (A2-r2); then the Transit and CBOR profiles as today. `grep    |\n|       |          |                                                                             | ':ws/\\(subprotocol\\|frame-kind\\|portable-value?\\|encode\\|decode\\)'` |\n|       |          |                                                                             | over `src/` hits only the two profiles, the seam key sites, and     |\n|       |          |                                                                             | `codec.cljc`'s fallback. A `remote-channel` spec naming a codec     |\n|       |          |                                                                             | is honoured end to end (one test per host). Fixtures pass.          |\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| C2    | migrate  | Every caller supplies its profile explicitly: `yin.repl.serve`/`connect`    | Three lanes green; counts unchanged plus the fixture-driven         |\n|       |          | pass `dao.stream.transit/profile`; every test in §7.2 names its profile;    | tests; `grep` for codec-less `make-attacher`/`make-endpoint`/       |\n|       |          | one parameterized fixture runs codec-neutral suites over both profiles;     | `listen!`/serve/dial calls over `src/` and `test/` returns none.    |\n|       |          | one-arity descriptor gates replaced by two-arity calls at every site.       | The §7.3 bidirectional old/new peer check runs here for both        |\n|       |          | Defaults still exist but nothing reaches them.                              | profiles. Fixtures pass.                                            |\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| C3    | contract | `dao.stream.ws` drops the Transit require, `checked-codec`'s default, the   | Three lanes green; counts unchanged plus: codec-less                |\n|       |          | one-arity gates, the `make-endpoint` default and the no-offer default       | `make-attacher`, `make-endpoint`, `listen!`/`handle-request!`       |\n|       |          | (refused). The four adapters drop `default-codecs`, the Transit require     | throw (one test per host); a seam without a name is refused;        |\n|       |          | and the `ws/subprotocol` fallback. `remote-channel` refuses `::no-codec`.   | `remote-channel` returns `::no-codec`. `grep dao.stream.transit     |\n|       |          |                                                                             | src/` hits only `transit*.clj*` and `yin/repl/{serve,connect}`.     |\n|       |          |                                                                             | `dao.stream.ws.md` \"absent name\" sentence amended; its              |\n|       |          |                                                                             | codec-profile text points here. Fixtures pass.                      |\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| C4    | contract | Contract dual acceptance to one key set. Runs only after OQ3 is resolved    | Three lanes green; counts unchanged except the dual-key gate        |\n|       |          | (§10), and which set survives is OQ3's answer. **If OQ3 moves the keys**    | tests of C0, which are **rewritten** (not deleted) in this slice:   |\n|       |          | (CD5): both profiles drop the `:ws/*` keys, accessors become single lookup, | each test that asserted \"a map complete under the other key set is  |\n|       |          | `profile?` accepts the `:dao.stream.codec/*` set only. **If OQ3 keeps       | accepted\" now asserts it is rejected, each test that asserted the   |\n|       |          | `:ws/*`**: both profiles drop the `:dao.stream.codec/*` keys, the dual      | fallback lookup now asserts a single lookup, and the count of       |\n|       |          | lookup is deleted, the accessors read `:ws/*`, `profile?` accepts the five  | gate tests before and after is equal and recorded. The grep gate    |\n|       |          | `:ws/*` keys plus one added `:ws/bytes?` boolean (the capability of §3.3    | is **conditional on OQ3**: under the move, `grep ':ws/\\(frame-kind  |\n|       |          | must survive either answer).                                                | \\|portable-value?\\|encode\\|decode\\|bytes?\\)'` over `src/` and       |\n|       |          |                                                                             | `test/` is empty and `:ws/subprotocol` survives only on seam maps;  |\n|       |          |                                                                             | under keep, `grep ':dao.stream.codec/'` over `src/` and `test/`     |\n|       |          |                                                                             | hits only the namespace's own name, and no absence of `:ws/*` is    |\n|       |          |                                                                             | asserted. Under both answers: fixtures pass; the §7.3 bidirectional |\n|       |          |                                                                             | check runs again. Slow tier once.                                   |\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| C5    | owner    | Owner-gated (§6.4): `yin.repl` selects `dao.stream.cbor/profile`;           | Same suites green over CBOR; `grep dao.stream.transit src/` hits    |\n|       |          | cross-host peer suites configured for CBOR.                                 | only `transit*.clj*`. Not commissioned by this document.            |\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nWhy each intermediate state is green. After C0 every consumer still reads\n`:ws/*` keys that are still present. After C1 every consumer reads through\naccessors that find either key set, and every default is still in place, so\na composition that names nothing behaves exactly as today. After C2 nothing\nreaches a default, so C3 can delete the defaults without changing any\nobservable path; C3 is the only slice whose acceptance includes a behaviour\nchange (codec-less compositions fail), and that change is confined to direct\ncompositions and tests, which C2 already migrated. C4 deletes keys nobody\nreads. The dependency is strictly linear: F0, C0, C1, C2, C3, C4.\n\nF0 through C4 depend on nothing outside this document. Other tracks may depend\non them (any track that needs `dao.stream.ws` free of a codec default); such a\ndependency is stated in the other track's document, never here.\n\n### 7.2 Test impact (V, grep of `test/` on 2026-10-20)\n\nNamespaces requiring `dao.stream.transit`: 22 by `grep -l dao.stream.transit\ntest/` (an earlier brief said 20; the grep count is authoritative):\n`dao.stream.ws-test`,\n`ws-codec-test` (`.cljc`, `.cljd`), `ws.jvm-test`, `ws.node-test`,\n`ws.browser-test`, `ws-project-peer`, `ws-project-cross-jvm-test`,\n`ws-project-cross-node-test`, `remote-route-test`, `loopback-net`,\n`transit-test`, `cbor-test` (`.cljc`, `.cljd`), `dao.jing.stream-test`,\n`yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `head-board-test`,\n`transfer-peer`, `yin.repl.main-test`, `yin.repl.slice-peer`. They keep\nrequiring the codec they exercise; that is composition.\n\nNamespaces that rely on a default and change in C2: `ws-test`\n(`make-attacher` without `:codec` at `:41,68,93,110,133,157,186`; `make-endpoint`\nwithout `:codecs` at `:207,262,521`; seams without a name at\n`:224-234,243,272,304,336,427,532-533,583`; one-arity gates at `:567-570`),\n`ws-codec-test` (both), `ws.jvm-test`, `ws.node-test`, `ws.dart-test`,\n`ws.browser-test`, `ws-project-test`, `ws-project-jvm-test`, `ws-project-peer`,\n`ws-project-cross-jvm-test`, `ws-project-cross-node-test`,\n`remote-channel-test` (`ws/descriptor?` one-arity at `:174`; serve/dial\nwithout codec), `loopback-net` (no seam name; Transit at `:164`),\n`yin.repl.host.jvm-test` (`:87-109`), and the yin.repl network tests through\nserve/connect. `transit-test` keeps its predicate cases (`transit_test.cljc:15-57`)\nunchanged, since Transit's predicate is unchanged; `codec-test` is new.\n`cbor-test` loses `transit-domain-stays-a-subset-of-the-cbor-domain` (both\nhosts) to the law test in C0 and keeps the rest; `journal-test`\n(`cbor/validate-portable`, `:354`) is unaffected. Exact per-lane counts are\nthe slice reports' to record.\n\n### 7.3 Wire proof: frozen fixtures and bidirectional peers\n\nReview finding A3: the cross-host peer suites run new code against new code,\nso both ends can drift together and \"wire unchanged\" would be asserted, not\nproved. The proof has two parts, both mandatory.\n\n**Frozen fixtures (F0).** Generated once from the pre-change tree at the\ntagged F0 commit and committed under `test/` as data files the three lanes\nall load (one directory per profile; CBOR payloads as hex, Transit payloads\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
